WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:08.240 --> 00:00:11.930
PROFESSOR: Hello,
welcome again to 8701.

00:00:11.930 --> 00:00:15.560
In this short video, I'll talk
about the books we are using

00:00:15.560 --> 00:00:18.670
and the literature we
are using in this class.

00:00:18.670 --> 00:00:21.050
So let's dive right into.

00:00:21.050 --> 00:00:23.510
There's a sequence
of textbooks I

00:00:23.510 --> 00:00:27.560
go back to when I prepare
the material for the class.

00:00:27.560 --> 00:00:29.270
You know, the one
which I use in order

00:00:29.270 --> 00:00:33.830
to derive the outline or
the schedule for the class

00:00:33.830 --> 00:00:37.730
is Introduction to High
Energy Physics by Perkins.

00:00:37.730 --> 00:00:41.210
But again, I use
material from a sequence

00:00:41.210 --> 00:00:47.300
of textbooks and reading
material for you guys as well.

00:00:47.300 --> 00:00:50.150
Nuclear physics is not
covered in Perkins,

00:00:50.150 --> 00:00:54.575
so we have here Samuel Wong's
book on Introductory Nuclear

00:00:54.575 --> 00:00:55.860
Physics.

00:00:55.860 --> 00:01:00.250
We spend about two weeks
talking about nuclear physics

00:01:00.250 --> 00:01:02.720
towards the second
part of the class.

00:01:02.720 --> 00:01:09.310
And a couple of basics, we talk
about the introductory material

00:01:09.310 --> 00:01:11.580
really in the sequence.

00:01:11.580 --> 00:01:13.940
A book I like a lot
is the Introduction

00:01:13.940 --> 00:01:17.750
to Elementary
Particles by Griffiths.

00:01:17.750 --> 00:01:22.970
And you see me using examples
out of that book a bit.

00:01:22.970 --> 00:01:27.260
Then on the nuclear physics
side there is Kenneth Krane.

00:01:27.260 --> 00:01:29.810
It's an MIT book
and a book which

00:01:29.810 --> 00:01:32.870
has been put together by
MIT faculty and research

00:01:32.870 --> 00:01:33.830
scientists.

00:01:33.830 --> 00:01:37.280
And then there's Techniques for
Nuclear and Particle Physics

00:01:37.280 --> 00:01:39.020
by Leo, which I like a lot.

00:01:39.020 --> 00:01:40.760
It's a little bit
of an older book,

00:01:40.760 --> 00:01:44.240
but it goes into some
of the technical details

00:01:44.240 --> 00:01:46.640
and material details which
are important to understand

00:01:46.640 --> 00:01:49.430
how we build detectors.

00:01:49.430 --> 00:01:52.330
And then a more recent book
is Modern Particle Physics

00:01:52.330 --> 00:01:53.660
by Mark Thomson.

00:01:53.660 --> 00:01:58.520
It dives right into particle
physics, the energy frontier.

00:01:58.520 --> 00:02:00.070
And it's really nice to read.

00:02:00.070 --> 00:02:00.890
It's a modern book.

00:02:00.890 --> 00:02:04.250
And it's easy to
read and comprehend.

00:02:04.250 --> 00:02:07.640
I recommend to have a look
at the review articles

00:02:07.640 --> 00:02:10.759
by the particle data group.

00:02:10.759 --> 00:02:14.120
They are really
concise articles which

00:02:14.120 --> 00:02:17.570
are for beginners or for
introductory level maybe

00:02:17.570 --> 00:02:19.640
a little bit
difficult. But as we

00:02:19.640 --> 00:02:21.450
go through the
material in this class,

00:02:21.450 --> 00:02:25.010
you should be able to take
those articles to review

00:02:25.010 --> 00:02:26.690
certain sections of this class.

00:02:26.690 --> 00:02:29.710
For example, QCD, or
electroweak interactions,

00:02:29.710 --> 00:02:31.070
the Higgs mechanism.

00:02:31.070 --> 00:02:33.440
And while you do
this, you also learn

00:02:33.440 --> 00:02:37.280
one of the latest results
and measurement in this area.

00:02:37.280 --> 00:02:41.330
I'll be posting a set of papers
as we go through the class.

00:02:41.330 --> 00:02:44.180
And you'll see in the
course organization

00:02:44.180 --> 00:02:46.100
that I'll ask you to
actually summarize

00:02:46.100 --> 00:02:49.760
some of those papers in
our recitation section.

00:02:49.760 --> 00:02:53.940
So those are going to be
important papers, for example,

00:02:53.940 --> 00:02:56.330
describing the
experiment which was used

00:02:56.330 --> 00:03:00.770
to measure parity violation,
or the paper on the Higgs

00:03:00.770 --> 00:03:03.080
discovery.

00:03:03.080 --> 00:03:04.880
That's it for literature.

00:03:04.880 --> 00:03:09.020
Please as always go ahead
and ask me questions.

00:03:09.020 --> 00:03:09.750
You know this.

00:03:09.750 --> 00:03:13.070
You know if you Google particle
physics or nuclear physics,

00:03:13.070 --> 00:03:16.310
you will find tons of
literature available

00:03:16.310 --> 00:03:17.750
on as many good books.

00:03:17.750 --> 00:03:19.640
And you might find
a different one

00:03:19.640 --> 00:03:23.330
from this listing which
suits your appetite

00:03:23.330 --> 00:03:25.570
for reading and learning.

